---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const failures = [
  ['Fabrication', 'A fact with no source at all, invented from nothing.', 'Less common than people expect, and the most alarming when it happens. Deterministic source checks catch it cleanly.'],
  ['Citation drift', 'A real source, bent to a number or claim it never made.', 'What you saw on stage. It’s the dangerous one because it survives a careful read.'],
  ['Overconfidence', 'A hedge-free answer to something genuinely uncertain.', 'Models don’t give you a confidence signal by default. Calibration and abstention need their own evals.'],
  ['Omission', 'What it left out: the counter-evidence, the caveat, the risk.', 'The hardest to test, because you’re checking for an absence.'],
  ['Nondeterminism', 'Same input, different answer tomorrow.', 'This is why you check every run. Pin what you can and measure variance on the rest.'],
  ['Sycophancy', 'It agrees with the framing in your prompt.', 'Leading questions produce agreeable, wrong answers. Test with adversarial phrasings.'],
  ['Silent regression', 'A model or prompt change quietly breaks what worked.', 'It’s why evals belong in CI. A suite that runs on every change catches it before your users do.'],
  ['Prompt injection', 'Text in the input hijacks the system’s instructions.', 'This one’s an input-side threat, so guardrails handle it rather than offline evals.'],
];

const taxonomy = [
  ['Groundedness / faithfulness', 'Can every claim name the exact sentence it came from?', 'Shown on stage: exact quote plus number presence. For open-ended RAG, entailment (NLI) or a judge with a rubric.'],
  ['Correctness / task success', 'Did it actually do the job that was asked?', 'Needs a reference or a checkable outcome. The clearest signal when you can define it.'],
  ['Completeness / schema', 'Is every required part present, in the required shape?', 'Cheap and deterministic. Validate the structure before you check anything semantic.'],
  ['Relevance / helpfulness', 'Is the answer on-point and useful, not just true?', 'Fuzzy. Usually a rubric with a judge or a human, and it’s where I see people over-trust judges most.'],
  ['Safety', 'Toxicity, PII leakage, resistance to being manipulated.', 'Classifiers + policy checks; overlaps with runtime guardrails.'],
  ['Robustness / adversarial', 'Does it hold up under hostile or edge-case inputs?', 'A red-team set of nasty cases, run like any other regression suite.'],
  ['Regression', 'Does the same set still pass after every change?', 'More a practice than a metric. The saved set is the asset, and pass/fail is the gate.'],
];

const interrogate = [
  ['Make it name the sentence.', 'For every fact, ask for the exact sentence from the source, word for word. If it can’t produce one, treat the fact as an assumption.'],
  ['Recompute the number yourself.', 'Any internal figure should be re-derivable from the data, so do the count yourself instead of trusting the stated total.'],
  ['Ask what would make it wrong.', 'A system that can’t state its own failure conditions hasn’t reasoned about them. Make it argue against itself.'],
  ['Check the sources are real.', 'Open two and confirm each page exists and says what was claimed. Plausible-looking citations are the easiest thing to fabricate.'],
  ['Run it twice and compare.', 'Same prompt, fresh session. Where the two answers disagree is where the uncertainty is hiding.'],
  ['Look for what’s missing.', 'Ask for the counter-argument, the caveat, and the do-nothing option by name. Omissions stay invisible until you do.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Evals in Real Life"
  description="Past the one check from the workshop: the kinds of evals, when to trust a model to judge, and the ones you'd run on a real AI analyst."
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-10">
    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 mb-3">
      <a href="/trust/" class="hover:underline">Build an AI Analyst You Can Trust</a> · going deeper
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-6">
      Evals in real life.
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
      The workshop showed one check on one document, which is the smallest honest version of the idea. This page
      covers the rest: what goes wrong with language models, the checks that catch it, when a model can grade
      another model, and the evals I'd run on the FreshCart analyst. The habit stays the same throughout. Every
      claim carries its source, and something goes and checks.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-4 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 text-sm">
      <div class="flex-1">
        <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Two ways to read this.</span>
        <span class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"> The plain text is for anyone who commissions AI work or signs off on it.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex-1">
        <span class="inline-block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 dark:text-primary-300 bg-primary-50 dark:bg-primary-950/40 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">Build</span>
        <span class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"> The blue notes go deeper for whoever engineers it.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 1. Atom -> molecule -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      One check was the atom. Here is the molecule.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-3">
      On stage, one deterministic check ran once against one brief and caught one invented statistic. That's the
      atom of the whole discipline: a claim, a source, and code that goes and verifies. A real system keeps the atom
      and scales it up. You check a <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">set</b> of cases instead of one, you run
      a <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">suite</b> of checks, and you run them at two moments: before you ship
      and while it's live.
    </p>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>The gate you saw is a single assertion. In production it becomes a versioned dataset of representative
      inputs with expected properties, a suite of independent checks scored per case, a CI job that fails the build
      on regression, and a sampling job that scores live traffic. The pass/fail is cheap. The <em>dataset</em> is
      what lasts, so treat it like code: review changes to it, and never let the model under test write its own test
      cases.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. What goes wrong -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      What actually goes wrong.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      You can't check for failures you haven't named. These are the ones I see reach real decisions. Most of them
      are the predictable result of a system that completes patterns under pressure.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
      <table class="w-full text-sm stack-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">Failure</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">In plain terms</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">What it takes to catch</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100 dark:divide-gray-800/60 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          {failures.map(([name, plain, tech]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{name}</td>
              <td data-label="In plain terms" class="px-4 py-3 align-top">{plain}</td>
              <td data-label="What it takes to catch" class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">{tech}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>Two more that bite in production and rarely show up in demos. <b>Goodhart</b>: once a metric becomes the
      target, the system optimizes the metric instead of the goal (a groundedness score climbs while the answers get
      evasive). <b>Eval-set contamination</b>: test cases leak into prompts, fine-tunes, or few-shot examples, and the
      suite goes green for the wrong reason. Rotate held-out cases and keep a private set the pipeline never sees.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Taxonomy -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      What you can check.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      "Eval" covers a family of questions, and each one gets answered a different way. You rarely need all of them.
      Pick the two or three that match how <em>your</em> system fails.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
      <table class="w-full text-sm stack-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">Kind of eval</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">The question it answers</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold align-top">How it's usually done</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100 dark:divide-gray-800/60 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          {taxonomy.map(([name, q, how]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white">{name}</td>
              <td data-label="The question it answers" class="px-4 py-3 align-top">{q}</td>
              <td data-label="How it's usually done" class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">{how}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. How you check + when each fails -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      How you check, and where each one fails.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      I group the ways to grade an answer into four. The skill is matching the method to the question, and knowing
      how the method itself fails.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">1 · Deterministic code</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">This is the check you built: fetch the source, look for the sentence, recompute the number. It's exact and cheap, and you can't talk it out of a verdict. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> what you care about can't be reduced to a rule, like tone, nuance, or "is this actually helpful."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">2 · Reference-based</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">Compare the answer to a known-good one. When a gold answer exists, the signal is clear. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> there's no single right answer, or when a correct response is worded differently from the reference.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">3 · A model as the judge</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">One model grades another's output against a rubric. It handles nuance that code can't touch. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> you trust it blindly. Judges have real biases, and they can be wrong in the same direction as the writer.</p>
        <div class="build mt-3">
          <span class="build-label">Build</span>
          <p>Known biases to design around: <b>position</b> (favors the first or last option), <b>verbosity</b> (favors longer answers), <b>self-preference</b> (favors its own style or family), and <b>correlated error</b> (misses exactly what the generator missed). Mitigations: score against an explicit rubric instead of vibes, calibrate the judge against human labels before you trust its numbers, randomize order and run pairwise, and use judges to <em>rank and triage</em> rather than to gate the safety-critical path. I keep the check that matters most deterministic, which is why the stage gate was code.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">4 · Human review</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">A person judges, and the other three methods are approximations of this. <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Fails when</b> you need it at scale or on every run, because humans skim and experts skim faster. Use people to calibrate the cheap checks rather than to be the check.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Two moments -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      Two moments: before you ship, and while it's live.
    </h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">Offline, before you ship</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Run the suite against your saved set every time anything changes: a prompt, a model, a tool. A red suite blocks the release. This is where the stage gate lives.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
        <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">Online, while it's live</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Sample real traffic and score it in production. Watch groundedness over time, not just at launch. They're different jobs. Passing offline doesn't mean it holds up on inputs you never imagined.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>The step people skip: <b>canary a model upgrade against the frozen suite before you cut over.</b> A newer model can score better on general benchmarks and still quietly regress your groundedness, so diff the suite before you switch. In production, alert on the trend rather than a single bad run.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Guardrails vs evals -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      Guardrails are not evals.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-3">
      I see people treat these as one thing and then wonder why one layer isn't enough.
      An <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">eval</b> is the crash test: it runs offline and measures quality against a set.
      A <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">guardrail</b> is the seatbelt: it runs at request time on the live input and
      output, and it can block or rewrite before the user sees anything. You need both. The crash test tells you
      it's safe to ship, and the seatbelt covers the ride you didn't test for.
    </p>
    <div class="build">
      <span class="build-label">Build</span>
      <p>Runtime guardrails: input filters (prompt-injection and jailbreak detection, PII scrubbing), output filters
      (toxicity, PII, policy, groundedness-at-inference on retrieved context), and refusal/abstention when confidence
      is low. Evals measure the system in aggregate, and guardrails defend a single request. A groundedness check can
      live in both: offline as a suite metric, online as a gate on the response.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Evals for this analyst -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      The evals you'd actually run on this analyst.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-5">
      Here's what I'd run on the FreshCart brief. You've already watched two of them run.
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3">
      {[
        ['Groundedness', 'Each web claim’s exact quote has to appear in the cited source. Deterministic.', 'shown'],
        ['Numeric recompute', 'Internal statistics get re-derived from the CSV instead of trusted as stated.', 'shown'],
        ['Completeness / schema', 'All five brief sections present; every claim carries a source field.', ''],
        ['Retrieval quality', 'The cited sources are real, reachable, and actually on topic.', ''],
        ['Scope', 'It read only the feedback file and the sources it cited. Nothing it wasn’t handed.', ''],
        ['Regression set', 'A handful of briefs with known-good outcomes, re-run on every prompt or model change.', ''],
      ].map(([name, desc, tag]) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-4">
          <span class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>
          <div>
            <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
              {name}
              {tag === 'shown' && <span class="ml-2 text-xs font-medium text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 uppercase tracking-wide">shown on stage</span>}
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-0.5">{desc}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. What the gate does NOT catch (centerpiece) -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-primary-200 dark:border-primary-900/60 bg-primary-50/50 dark:bg-primary-950/20 p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
        What the gate does <em>not</em> catch.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-4">
        This is the part I most want you to take away. Even a perfect groundedness gate only catches a fabricated
        citation. It won't catch:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed list-disc ml-5 max-w-2xl">
        <li>A <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">real source bent to the wrong conclusion</b>, where every sentence checks out and the argument is still false.</li>
        <li><b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Omitted counter-evidence</b>, like the study it didn't cite because it undercut the recommendation.</li>
        <li>A <b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">reasoning error</b>: sound facts, invalid inference.</li>
        <li><b class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">The question it should have asked</b> and didn't.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mt-4">
        No single eval covers all of these. That's why you run a suite, why you add human judgment for what code
        can't reach, and why a person still signs off. The gate takes away the failure people are worst at catching
        (the confident, well-sourced, wrong number), so the person signing can spend their judgment where only
        judgment works.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Interrogate -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-14 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      How to interrogate any output.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-6">
      These are the checklist's four questions turned into six moves you can make on a single answer, before you build
      any of this. Do them by hand first, then automate the ones that repeat.
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-3">
      {interrogate.map(([q, a], i) => (
        <li class="flex gap-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 p-5">
          <span class="text-2xl font-bold font-serif text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 tabular-nums leading-none shrink-0 w-8">{i + 1}</span>
          <div>
            <div class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">{q}</div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed text-sm">{a}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. Close -->
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
    <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white font-serif mb-4">
      The habit stays the same as the toolkit grows.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-4">
      Every technique on this page runs the same rule (every claim carries its source, and something goes and
      checks) at more scale, against more failure modes, and at more moments. My advice is to start with the one
      deterministic check that catches your worst failure, and add the next one when a real miss earns it. Reach for
      a model-as-judge only where code can't do the job, and never for the decision that matters most.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Tooling for all of this exists (eval harnesses, tracing and observability, guardrail runtimes), and it changes
      every year. The principle outlasts any of it: name your failures, save your set, fail closed, and keep the
      receipt.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a class="btn-solid" href="/trust/">Back to the workshop</a>
      <a class="btn-outline" href="https://github.com/kzaky/freshcart-agent" rel="noopener">Get the repo</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  .build { margin-top: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-primary-500); background: var(--color-primary-50); border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; }
  .dark .build { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-primary-950) 35%, transparent); }
  .build p { font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-gray-700); margin: 0; }
  .dark .build p { color: var(--color-gray-300); }
  .build em { font-style: italic; }
  .build-label { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--color-primary-700); margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .dark .build-label { color: var(--color-primary-300); }
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    table.stack-sm thead { display: none; }
    table.stack-sm tr { display: block; padding: 0.75rem 0; }
    table.stack-sm td { display: block; padding: 0.25rem 1rem; }
    table.stack-sm td[data-label]::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 600; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-gray-400); margin-top: 0.25rem; }
  }
  .btn-solid { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; border-radius: 0.5rem; background: var(--color-primary-600); color: #fff; padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; text-decoration: none; }
  .btn-solid:hover { background: var(--color-primary-700); }
  .btn-outline { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; border-radius: 0.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-gray-300); padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-gray-900); text-decoration: none; }
  .btn-outline:hover { background: var(--color-gray-50); }
  .dark .btn-outline { border-color: var(--color-gray-700); color: #fff; }
  .dark .btn-outline:hover { background: var(--color-gray-900); }
</style>
